#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1a5e8fef-3361-5d24-9abd-d0474a2cd3ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-1141" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6ca63cd3e05b74666259ef1696e51f702f6fb86f770ff84ca3b0ce7c3cd74744.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between the displacement $d,$ in centimeters, of a particular spring when a mass $m,$ in kilograms, is attached to it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $d$ and $m.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $d$ varies directly with $m,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nd = km.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $m=5$ and $d=20.$ So, we substitute this data into the above equation to find $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =k\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{20}{5} & =k \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nd=4m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32650c78-38d5-5530-991d-bf0b8f2198cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graphs of Proportional Relationships"}] :question/id #uuid "42e81da3-7ea4-5b58-9163-3057df064c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-4460" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below represent proportional relationships?\n\n![](images/4c/4cc220afc9f6b1f8752076d0b8d8233caec22e937c0579926439b39472be8c80.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nOf the given graphs, only graph II fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I and IV are not straight lines.\n\n- Graph III does not pass through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "06bdefa1-8b40-5777-9ca0-9f257834833d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4461" :question/problem "The graph below shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?\n\n![](images/62/6298e9ee004e642d05d065d1fc2fd325f07d313ac736ef15b190c1b9c90d1019.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,3).$\n\n![](images/d5/d573d9aad231622b2e5dc42a8672d973e5fda518d75894f3bafb0b1a97532d24.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d599015-2a54-5f7e-85f1-92d68d712b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498023} {:answer/id #uuid "70eb4018-3e01-5fcb-8bd7-41b5b8f99d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498070} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe531c2-2b22-5b4c-ad95-b3c61682f842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498217} {:answer/id #uuid "e456fe40-fb0d-5e53-a598-99c236b140b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=-\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498307} {:answer/id #uuid "eed48361-6692-5f6f-bbb6-806e1e1e0986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d599015-2a54-5f7e-85f1-92d68d712b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498023} :answer-field/id #uuid "34713ef1-1946-5bb9-ad47-3d471b85de5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f89b123-1c77-5362-ac5f-d71cbdc1fae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115616" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3de064ab8dbab633656b19cfc340f6ce43f4df8a7e18834019e6301ef67ab00.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $h$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $h$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nh=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $h=6$ and $x=3.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =k\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =k \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nh=2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32650c78-38d5-5530-991d-bf0b8f2198cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graphs of Proportional Relationships"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ec417b-980a-5d17-a5b9-12017396fe87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497818} {:answer/id #uuid "6d65c56f-