 the origin $(0,0).$\n\nOf the given graphs, none of the graphs fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and III  are not straight lines.\n\n- Graph  IV does not pass through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5156af9d-0f97-54a7-b538-83817e76d2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497992} {:answer/id #uuid "588087e5-d99b-57ff-aa86-b80cd2659ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{9}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498007} {:answer/id #uuid "b50ea358-f56c-5009-965d-883747116985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498203} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b54beb-88be-5a00-b4be-0e0ed80986df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{15}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498206} {:answer/id #uuid "c923d2a2-8273-5dac-82a6-4b2cd9a07dfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{16}{3}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6b54beb-88be-5a00-b4be-0e0ed80986df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{15}{4}$ gallons per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498206} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6cb29c1-d018-56dc-816a-3dbf5cb4e4f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ada853a-eb91-5071-a9de-8ca7d83b9586" :question/math-academy-id "q-163004" :question/problem "![](images/17/1778ff7ea1ee5b1bcf918992d8b2be700362031cd7d142b00b3dc79470983eb0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) that water flows through a hose into a tank and the corresponding amount $y$ of water (in gallons) that has entered the tank during that period. What is the unit rate of the water flow?" :question/worked-solution "The unit rate in gallons per minute is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,15).$\n\n![](images/d0/d063a3c2ebf77d1132349b7e48f883632d97720d491a4a5c9158b49c5809258e.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water flow unit rate is $\\frac{15}{4}$ gallons per minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a9528e-1c2c-5770-b44f-a6e4d3c1e7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=240t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497839} {:answer/id #uuid "29f1ebd2-840a-5666-b57c-40488ad0dbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=210t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497886} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf3586b-e955-5c1c-9ff9-3fedb68db294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=100t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497891} {:answer/id #uuid "618998ae-77ec-5ed8-a5b6-fa95b0f274d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=800t+40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498030} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d054bd-4520-536a-9353-5c16dda8e80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=840t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29f1ebd2-840a-5666-b57c-40488ad0dbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=210t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497886} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef46a245-0a9f-58d0-8e08-5d3259ec7510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "700640db-e0fe-571f-9cca-6b3afdd864a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51877" :question/problem "![](images/47/472c0aebabed979b97d9d68648785773f0715da9e8cf88c1f1ee912a27d6cb77.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the distance $d$ an electric train travels is directly proportional to the number of hours $t$ since its journey started. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $d$ and $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $d$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nd=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=4$ and $d=840.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}840 & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{840}{4} & =k \\\\ 210 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nd=210t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d25023-34e5-5862-8bea-4fc160f76af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497846} {:answer/id #uuid "794140a2-4957-5b8c-b260-1cc5c6c4bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498090} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4b8fe6-c15c-5921-a99c-5eee21236872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498214} {:answer/id #uuid "de8ebc30-9cde-5db4-8cb3-76297608fe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498297} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9dbe99-1bc2-5f94-bad7-f72a4944703f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "794140a2-4957-5b8c-b260-1cc5c6c4bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498090} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c3938ba-3b32-5529-8edd-a993ae710193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "476b2500-a5bd-5785-964a-0d0c4c0bd744" :question/math-academy-id "q-91754" :question/problem "![](images/df/df0b1950f3093cf368cdd463b1c137df98bae4eb29d7d51f2ce7b34a17c93c77.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?" :question/worked-solution "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(5,4).$\n\n![](images/46/468695422e5c641a076c8f2889f926a7c633c69e98c96fdefb07b2c19c2acbdc.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}]}
